9;ing Period/Chin Sh\u00eang-t&#39;an\">Chin Sh\u00eang-t'an</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jin_Shenghuan\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Jin Shenghuan\">Wikipedia article</a>, <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367116\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367116\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3635480</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 1 \u2014 Chin Sh\u00eang-huan</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">Earl Swisher</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"166\" data-page-number=\"166\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/178\" data-page-index=\"178\" data-page-quality=\"4\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/178\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;178\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>CHIN</b> Sh\u00eang-huan \u91d1\u8072\u6853 (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u864e\u81e3), d. Mar. 1, 1649, soldier under both the Ming and Ching dynasties, was a native of Liaotung. He was a brigade-general under the Ming general, Huang Lung \u9ec3\u9f8d, when the latter was defeated and killed in 1633 by the Manchus at L\u00fc-shun, Liaotung. His wife and son were taken captive, but he himself managed to escape and join the forces of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Tso_Liang-y%C3%BC\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Tso Liang-y\u00fc\">Tso Liang-y\u00fc</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], in Honan. In 1645, as a subordinate of Tso M\u00eang-k\u00eang (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Tso_Liang-y%C3%BC\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Tso Liang-y\u00fc\">Tso Liang-y\u00fc</a></span>), he surrendered to the Ch'ing general, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Ajige\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Ajige\">Ajige</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], at Kiukiang, offered his services to the new dynasty, and was appointed general-in-chief of Kiangsi, specially charged with the suppression of Ming loyalists. He subjugated the whole of Kiangsi province without the help of Manchu troops, and these successful operations against the southern Ming were acknowledged with imperial honors. His family was released, but his demand for wide discretionary powers in civil and military affairs was regarded by the Boards at Peking as presumptious. He&#32;<span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"167\" data-page-number=\"167\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/179\" data-page-index=\"179\" data-page-quality=\"4\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/179\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;179\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span>made an almost regal court of his military headquarters at Nanchang, and entertained Taoist magicians who nursed his growing resentment against the Manchus. His subordinate, Wang T\u00ea-j\u00ean \u738b\u5f97\u4ec1, nicknamed Wang the Piebald \u738b\u96dc\u6bdb, a former follower of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Li_Tz%C5%AD-ch%27%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch&#39;\u00eang\">Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], encouraged his feeling that his services required a marquisate.\n</p><p>In 1648 Chin rebelled, killed the governor, and declared allegiance to <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chu_Yu-lang\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chu Yu-lang\">Chu Yu-lang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] who confirmed on him his self-conferred title, Duke Y\u00fc-kuo \u8c6b\u570b\u516c. He utilized the services of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chiang_Y%C3%BCeh-kuang\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chiang Y\u00fceh-kuang\">Chiang Y\u00fceh-kuang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] who three years previously had quarreled with <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Ma_Shih-ying\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Ma Shih-ying\">Ma Shih-ying</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] and had left the court of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chu_Yu-sung\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chu Yu-sung\">Chu Yu-sung</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] at Nanking. Failing in his attempt to take Kan-chou, he was besieged at Nanchang by the Manchu commander, Tantai (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Yangg%C3%BBri_efu\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yangg\u00fbri efu\">Yangg\u00fbri</a></span>). This siege Chin withstood for eleven months, but when the city fell (March 1, 1649) he was wounded by an arrow and drowned himself in a pond east of the city wall. The posthumous title, Prince of Nanchang \u5357\u660c\u738b, and the name, Chuang-wu \u58ef\u6b66, were conferred by the Ming court. His associate, Wang T\u00ea-j\u00ean, was captured and killed.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[M.1/271/16a; M.41/75/4a, 16/3b; M.59/65/5a; \u660e\u5b63\u5357\u7565 <i>Ming-chi nan-l\u00fceh</i> 13/15a; \u4e09\u85e9\u8a18\u4e8b\u672c\u672b <i>San-fan chi-shih p\u00ean-mo</i> 2/8b, 3/5a in \u501f\u6708\u5c71\u623f\u5f59\u9214 <i>Chieh-y\u00fceh shan-fang hui-ch'ao</i> ;\u897f\u5357\u8a18\u4e8b <i>Hsi-nan chi-shih</i> 11/1a in \u90b5\u6b66\u5f90\u6c0f\u53e2\u66f8 <i>Shao-wu Hs\u00fc-shih ts'ung-shu</i>.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Earl Swisher</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Ajige"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chiang Y\u00fceh-kuang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chin Pao"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chin Sh\u00eang-t'an"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chu Yu-lang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chu Yu-sung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Ma Shih-ying"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Tso Liang-y\u00fc"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yangg\u00fbri efu"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Earl Swisher"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 1\n | onlysection = chin sheng-huan\n | previous = Chin Pao\n | next     = Ch